WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.325
Building a small labeled retrieval set.

2
00:00:02.605 --> 00:00:06.509
None of the comparisons in this week mean anything without a labeled set.

3
00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:19.568
It doesn’t need to be large to be useful: a few dozen to a couple of hundred queries is enough to catch big regressions and compare configurations, though small differences between configurations will be within the noise.

4
00:00:19.848 --> 00:00:26.995
Hamel Husain’s writing on evals makes the general case well: look at real data, and build the evaluation from it.

5
00:00:27.275 --> 00:00:30.155
For retrieval, that means: Collect real queries.

6
00:00:30.435 --> 00:00:34.659
Search logs, support tickets, questions people ask in team channels.

7
00:00:34.939 --> 00:00:41.168
If you have no traffic yet, ask the people who will use the system to write the questions they’d actually ask.

8
00:00:41.448 --> 00:00:48.403
Cover the query types from lesson 7: identifiers, how-to questions, policy questions, vague questions.

9
00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:50.581
Label what would answer each query.

10
00:00:50.861 --> 00:01:01.229
For each query, find the passages that contain the answer, and grade them if you’re using nDCG (for example 2 = fully answers, 1 = partially).

11
00:01:01.509 --> 00:01:13.541
The fastest workflow is to pool candidates from several retrievers (BM25, vectors, hybrid) and label the union, then search by hand for queries where nothing relevant came up.

12
00:01:13.821 --> 00:01:15.656
Anchor labels to something stable.

13
00:01:15.936 --> 00:01:22.699
If you label chunk ids, your labels break the moment you change chunking, which is precisely when you most need them.

14
00:01:22.979 --> 00:01:30.339
Label a document id plus the answering text span, and map spans to whatever chunks the current pipeline produces.

15
00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:32.432
Include queries with no answer.

16
00:01:32.712 --> 00:01:35.635
Some real questions aren’t covered by your corpus.

17
00:01:35.915 --> 00:01:46.133
They have no relevant items, so retrieval metrics don’t apply to them, but they’re essential for testing whether the full system declines to answer (Studio 2’s week 4 topic).

18
00:01:46.413 --> 00:01:47.523
Check the labels.

19
00:01:47.803 --> 00:01:51.451
Have a second person label a sample, and discuss disagreements.

20
00:01:51.731 --> 00:01:55.635
Where people disagree, the query is ambiguous, and that’s worth knowing.

21
00:01:55.915 --> 00:02:00.395
You can use a model to draft synthetic queries from chunks to bootstrap a set.

22
00:02:00.675 --> 00:02:11.683
Treat them with care: a question generated from a chunk tends to reuse that chunk’s wording, which flatters keyword search and makes retrieval look easier than real user queries are.

23
00:02:11.963 --> 00:02:15.952
Mix in real queries, and report metrics on the two groups separately.
